From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: ucsi: huawei_gaokun: Retry the initialization
 after a timeout

On some boots ucsi_init() fails with -ETIMEDOUT ("PPM init failed"),
usually after "failed to register alt modes": the EC answers
GET_ALTERNATE_MODES with BUSY, then with COMMAND_COMPLETE but BUSY still
set, and leaves BUSY set. The core ignores a CCI with BUSY set, so the
command times out after 10 s and UCSI stays down until the next boot. In
the failures logged with the EC traffic, the commands sent after it
completed normally.

Set UCSI_RETRY_INIT_ON_TIMEOUT. When a retry succeeds, the ports of such
a boot should come up about 15 s later than on a normal boot, depending
on which command timed out, instead of staying down.

The check for a failed initialization, which acknowledges USB events
once nothing else will, keeps working: a queued retry counts as busy, so
it holds only after the last retry has failed. Update its comment.

Tested with timeouts simulated by a test-only change: two failures of
GET_ALTERNATE_MODES, after which the third run registered the ports and
the USB HAL reported port0; one failure that first waited 10 s, so that
the ports came up after boot had completed, and the USB HAL reported
port0 again; and six failures of GET_CAPABILITY, after which the driver
stopped trying. None of 20 boots without simulation failed, so a retry
after a real failure has not been observed yet.

Fixes: 00327d7f2c8c ("usb: typec: ucsi: add Huawei Matebook E Go ucsi driver")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c | 17 ++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c b/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c
index 2433e69ba15..2351da067c5 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c
@@ -440,11 +440,12 @@ static void gaokun_ucsi_handle_usb_mode(struct gaokun_ucsi_port *port)
 
 /*
  * Whether UCSI has given up initializing. ucsi_register() only queues
- * ucsi->work; ucsi_init() runs from it and sets ucsi->connector on success.
- * On failure it leaves ucsi->connector NULL and does not try again, except
- * for -EPROBE_DEFER, which queues the work once more. work_busy() is only a
- * snapshot, so the caller acts on this only when it holds for two USB
- * events in a row.
+ * ucsi->work, and ucsi_init() runs from it. When it fails, it leaves
+ * ucsi->connector NULL, unless only the CCI read at its end failed. The work
+ * is queued again only after -EPROBE_DEFER, -EAGAIN or, with
+ * UCSI_RETRY_INIT_ON_TIMEOUT, -ETIMEDOUT, each a limited number of times; a
+ * queued retry counts as busy. work_busy() is only a snapshot, so the caller
+ * acts on this only when it holds for two USB events in a row.
  */
 static bool gaokun_ucsi_init_failed(struct gaokun_ucsi *uec)
 {
@@ -692,6 +693,12 @@ static int gaokun_ucsi_probe(struct auxiliary_device *adev,
 	if (IS_ERR(uec->ucsi))
 		return PTR_ERR(uec->ucsi);
 
+	/*
+	 * The EC sometimes completes a command but leaves BUSY set in the CCI,
+	 * so the command times out. It takes the next command normally.
+	 */
+	uec->ucsi->quirks |= UCSI_RETRY_INIT_ON_TIMEOUT;
+
 	ucsi_set_drvdata(uec->ucsi, uec);
 	auxiliary_set_drvdata(adev, uec);
 
